== History ==
Anti-logging protests were led by conservation activists Stephen King, Shirley Guildford, and others in the late 1970s in what is now Pureora Forest Park. They had a novel way of erecting platforms on treetops, sitting over it to protest logging operations in the forests. The result of their efforts was tri-fold: the park was established in 1978; the New Zealand Government changed rules to meet the protesters' demand to permanently stop logging operations; and the Native Forest Restoration Trust was formed which ensured that the park develops several areas into its present format. One of the pine forest areas that was restored...","coordinates":[{"lat":-38.61666667,"lon":175.53333333,"primary":true,"globe":"earth"}],"pageimage":"Te_Pureora_forest_1.jpg","pageprops":{"page_image_free":"Te_Pureora_forest_1.jpg","wikibase-shortdesc":"Protected rainforest park in New Zealand","wikibase_item":"Q7261180"}}]}}
